{"id":136,"date":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/bought-your-first-house-in-coatesville-read-this-before-winter\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:57","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:57","slug":"bought-your-first-house-in-coatesville-read-this-before-winter","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/bought-your-first-house-in-coatesville-read-this-before-winter\/","title":{"rendered":"Sharing Walls Means Sharing Heat\u2014Coatesville&#8217;s Steel-Era Math"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-cover alignfull s-hero-cover\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:2rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:2rem\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-contrast-background-color has-background-dim-70 has-background-dim\"><\/span><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"697\" class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background wp-image-198\" alt=\"brick rowhouse street\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b.jpg\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b-300x204.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b-768x523.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-cover-is-layout-ff0c90b8 wp-block-cover-is-layout-constrained\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center has-base-color has-text-color s-hero-cover__title wp-block-heading\">Shared walls, separate thermostats<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center s-lead has-base-color has-text-color has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">When steel workers shared walls, they unknowingly shared heat, and that physics still governs energy bills a century after the mills closed.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Get Your Numbers<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"#content\">Learn More<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why rowhouses heat differently<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>End units lose more heat \u2014 Three exterior walls instead of one. An end rowhouse bleeds heat through the side while middle units stay protected by neighbors.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Shared walls transfer heat both ways \u2014 If your neighbor keeps their home at 62\u00b0F, warmth flows through the party wall into their space. You pay to heat their home.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Vertical stratification gets worse \u2014 Heat rises. Basement stays cold while upstairs roasts. Narrow floor plans make duct routing difficult\u2014most rowhouses have undersized supply to upper floors.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Furnace sizing needs adjustment \u2014 Standard square-footage calculations fail. A middle-unit rowhouse needs less capacity than an end unit of identical size because three walls aren&#039;t exposed to weather.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1.png\" alt=\"furnace - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1757\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Heating system upgrades for attached housing<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most Coatesville rowhouses were converted from coal heat to natural gas decades ago, but conversion quality varies wildly. Some received proper forced-air installations with ducted supply to all floors. Others have makeshift systems where a gas furnace was dropped into a coal cavity with minimal duct modifications, leaving second floors chronically starved for heat. The ideal system for your rowhouse depends on its position in the block.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">End units with maximum exposure often benefit from zone heating\u2014supplemental electric baseboard in cold rooms or ductless mini-splits that offset lateral heat loss. Middle units with front-and-back exposure can thrive with a properly-sized and ducted forced-air system, though return air paths matter enormously in homes where walls have been added or removed over decades.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For rowhouses with finished plaster interiors and no ductwork space, high-velocity mini-duct systems use 2-inch flexible tubing snaked through wall cavities. These deliver conditioned air to rooms that have been poorly served for decades, using higher air pressure to compensate for smaller duct diameter. Installation costs $3,000\u2013$5,000 more than conventional systems but often the only practical path to whole-house comfort without tearing out historic finishes.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Four cooling approaches for rowhouse layouts<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Central air in existing ductwork \u2014 Condenser outside plus evaporator coil above furnace. If your heating ducts run well, AC is straightforward. Cost: $4,000\u2013$6,000. Rowhouses need smaller tonnage because shared walls don&#039;t gain solar heat.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductless mini-split single zone \u2014 One outdoor condenser, one indoor wall-mounted head. Cools the primary living space. Back-alley placement common in rowhouse blocks. Cost: $2,500\u2013$3,500. No ductwork required.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductless mini-split two-zone \u2014 One condenser serving a first-floor head and a second-floor bedroom unit. Targeted cooling where people spend time; upper bedrooms stay ambient. Cost: $4,000\u2013$5,500. Most popular rowhouse AC retrofit.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Portable window units supplemented \u2014 Not ideal long-term, but viable for renters or short-term owners. Lower upfront cost. Energy efficiency poor. Noise and privacy impact on tight blocks where neighbors share outdoor space.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Condenser placement in tight neighborhood blocks<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Coatesville&#039;s rowhouse blocks were built in clusters where outdoor space is minimal. Condenser units need clearance for airflow and service\u2014you can&#039;t wedge a unit into a narrow alley and expect it to function. Placement options in typical rowhouse lots: front yard (visible, causes noise issues with sidewalk pedestrians), back yard (most common, but alley access tight), or side yard if the lot width allows.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Back-alley condenser placement is standard in Coatesville rowhouses because it&#039;s hidden from street view and keeps the unit away from the main living space. The trade-off: your neighbors who share the alley hear the outdoor unit run during warm weather. This creates a courtesy conversation\u2014it&#039;s worth having it upfront rather than installing equipment that creates ongoing friction on a block where you see the same families year after year.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Service access matters as much as noise. A technician needs to reach the condenser, add refrigerant, or replace components. If it&#039;s boxed in tight, maintenance becomes expensive because the unit has to be partially uninstalled for work. Keystone Comfort Co.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">designs rowhouse AC systems with both noise minimization and service access in mind, sometimes using vibration-damping pads or quiet-operation-mode settings on modern units to reduce impact on neighbors who share your thermal ecosystem.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-condenser-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"condenser - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1483\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-condenser-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-condenser-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-condenser-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-condenser-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Envelope improvements that benefit rowhouses<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Building envelope work in rowhouses delivers uneven returns because of thermal coupling with neighbors. A new window in an exterior wall helps substantially. Insulation in a shared party wall helps less because heat still transfers through to the neighbor regardless of interior insulation quality. Prioritization matters\u2014some improvements benefit you fully, others are partially wasted on the neighbor&#039;s cold side.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Attic insulation to R-50 or higher\u2014not shared with anyone, full benefit is yours alone<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Air sealing around basement rim joists, door frames, and attic penetrations\u2014stops heat loss you alone benefit from stopping<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Front and back window replacement\u2014exterior walls aren&#039;t shared, improvements deliver full energy and comfort gains<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Avoid mid-wall cavity insulation alone\u2014heat transfer through party walls continues unless neighbors improve too<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Coordinated improvement with adjacent owners can mutually benefit both homes by reducing two-way party wall heat transfer<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The economics of improving a rowhouse you plan to keep<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Investing in HVAC and envelope work for a Coatesville rowhouse requires different financial thinking than detached homes. A high-efficiency furnace and new windows won&#039;t deliver full theoretical energy savings if neighbors keep their units significantly cooler because shared-wall heat transfer continues regardless. Property value recovery is uncertain\u2014appraisals in post-industrial neighborhoods often ignore system quality in favor of location and square footage.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A homeowner who spends $15,000 on heating upgrades and new windows may not see that full investment reflected in resale price. The most rational approach: prioritize improvements that deliver comfort and reliability at reasonable cost, not maximum efficiency or resale value recovery. A mid-efficiency furnace properly sized and ducted delivers far more comfort than an oversized high-efficiency unit poorly installed.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Insulation and air sealing reduce drafts and improve livability even if energy savings are modest. Ductless mini-splits for cooling provide substantial quality-of-life improvement for $4,000\u2013$6,000. Make decisions based on improving your daily living experience, not on expectations of cost recovery at sale. That&#039;s the Keystone Comfort Co. philosophy for rowhouse owners planning to stay long-term.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--ember\">\n<li>Steel-era party walls: interior units lose heat primarily through roof and floor<\/li>\n\n\n<li>PECO territory: time-of-use rates favor heat pump operation during off-peak hours<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Lincoln Highway corridor: traffic particulates require MERV-11+ filtration for equipment longevity<\/li>\n\n\n<li>1920s balloon framing: fire-stopping required before any ductwork modification<\/li>\n\n\n<li>End units 30%+ higher heating bills: three exposed walls vs. one in middle rowhouses<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Check these common rowhouse problems:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>End unit significantly colder than neighbors\u2014three exposed walls vs. one means different heat loss math<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Gas bill 40%+ higher than identical-looking rowhouse\u2014envelope differences between same-era homes compound<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Furnace works great mild winters but struggles below 20\u00b0F\u2014sized for average, not extreme cold<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Second floor hot in summer, cold in winter\u2014duct losses or inadequate supply to upper floors<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Basement stays damp despite dehumidifier running\u2014foundation moisture exceeding unit capacity<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">What we offer for Coatesville rowhouses<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-3 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Heating system replacement<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Properly sized furnace with ducting that actually serves upper floors. Zone heating if you&#039;re an end unit. We size for your block position, not just square footage.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductless cooling retrofit<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mini-split condenser hidden in back alley, indoor heads where you live. No tearing out plaster. Cost-effective AC for homes without duct space. Two-zone most popular in rowhouses.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Pressure testing and diagnostics<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We find where your rowhouse is losing heat\u2014shared wall leaks, undersized ducts, poor return air paths. Digital imaging shows the problems. Then we fix them.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Air sealing and insulation<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Basement rim joist sealing, attic insulation upgrades, door frame weatherization. These don&#039;t get wasted on neighbors. Full benefit stays in your home.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Furnace maintenance programs<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Annual tune-ups, filter changes, seasonal inspections. Catches problems before they strand you in January. Service calls within 24 hours on our maintenance customers.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Emergency service calls<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Furnace down in winter? We&#039;re available nights and weekends. Most rowhouse emergencies are duct blockages or circulation issues\u2014fixable fast once diagnosed correctly.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Rowhouse heating questions from Coatesville homeowners<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--numbered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my rowhouse heating bill seem higher than my neighbor&#039;s even though our homes look identical?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">End-unit rowhouses lose heat through three exterior walls while middle units have only two. An end unit&#039;s furnace cycles constantly to compensate. Additionally, if neighbors keep their homes significantly cooler, your heating system transfers warmth through shared walls\u2014you&#039;re partially heating their homes. Thermal coupling means your energy investment subsidizes their lower temperatures.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I install central air conditioning in a rowhouse that only has old radiator heating?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes. Two approaches: (1) High-velocity mini-duct system using 2-inch flexible tubing snaked through existing wall cavities\u2014minimal demolition, costs $5,000\u2013$7,000. (2) Ductless mini-split system with outdoor condenser and wall-mounted indoor heads\u2014no ductwork at all, costs $3,500\u2013$5,500, often easier to install in finished rowhouses. Both work well; choice depends on your ductwork space and aesthetic preferences.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Does improving insulation in my rowhouse help if my neighbors haven&#039;t improved theirs?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, but with caveats. Attic insulation and air sealing deliver full benefit\u2014they&#039;re not affected by neighbor behavior. Window replacement on exterior walls helps substantially. Party wall insulation alone provides reduced benefit because heat still transfers through to a cooler neighbor. The economics work best if you plan to stay long-term and value comfort gains, not energy cost recovery.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What size furnace does a rowhouse actually need?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Standard square-footage sizing formulas oversize rowhouse systems because they don&#039;t account for shared-wall thermal coupling. A 1,400 sq ft middle-unit rowhouse might only need 40,000 BTU\/hour instead of the 60,000 a detached home of that size requires. End units need more. We assess your specific position in the block, exposure, insulation, and occupant behavior to right-size the system. Oversizing wastes money and creates humidity problems.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my second floor stay cold in winter even when the furnace runs constantly?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Undersized ductwork to upper floors or poor return air paths. Rowhouses&#039; narrow width and complicated floor layouts mean HVAC wasn&#039;t originally designed for them. Supply vents might be closing at night, or return air is being blocked by walls. We run ductwork diagnostics\u2014blower door testing and duct pressure imaging\u2014to find the blockage. Often fixable by relocating return air grilles or adding a second return path.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-quote s-quote--accent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>The old steelworker row homes along Strode Avenue were converted to forced air in the seventies with undersized ducts run through closets. PECO bills run thirty percent above average because the systems fight restricted airflow every heating season. We work these blocks weekly.<\/p><cite>Keystone Comfort Co. field notes<\/cite><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-stats is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">3<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Exterior walls on end-unit rowhouses (vs. 2 on middle units)<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">30%<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Higher heating bills for end rowhouses at identical temperatures<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">$4,000\u2013$6,000<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Cost of central AC retrofit in rowhouse with existing ducts<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">2\u20133<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Preferred mini-split zones for rowhouse cooling<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">R-50<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Target attic insulation (delivers full efficiency benefit)<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rowhouse heating and cooling assessment<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If your row home heats unevenly\u2014first floor roasting while bedrooms stay cold\u2014a pressure test and visual inspection shows us exactly why. We diagnose problems other contractors miss because they don&#039;t understand party-wall physics.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule a rowhouse assessment<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-2 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" data-id=\"1476\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1476\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-gener